emo-http-server/config.yaml — aborting`.", "Why? The failing ReplicaSet mounts ConfigMap `gateway-config-bad`, whose `config.yaml` contains `invalid_directive: true`; the healthy ReplicaSet mounts `gateway-config` without that directive.", "Root cause: Invalid application configuration in `demo-ingress/ConfigMap/gateway-config-bad`; replace/remove the invalid directive or update the Deployment to the validated `gateway-config`."], "dueDiligence": {"targetAccuracy": "The target is `ConfigMap/gateway-config-bad`, whose data directly causes the startup failure and can be corrected without targeting the symptom-reporter Deployment. Resource context identifies the same ConfigMap as root owner.", "proportionality": "A single shared ConfigMap is the most specific remediable resource: correcting it fixes all three failing replicas and permits the Deployment rollout to recover. Node pressure is listed as a contributor rather than the primary target because it does not match the direct, evidenced failure mechanism.", "scopeCompleteness": "Inspected the Deployment, both ReplicaSets, all three failing pods, a healthy representative pod, both referenced ConfigMaps, namespace events, node usage/statistics, Service, and Ingress listing. Prometheus and Alertmanager were attempted but inaccessible due to timeouts. Downstream external clients were not inspected because the application fails before serving traffic.", "causalCompleteness": "The chain was traced from CrashLoopBackOff through exit code 1, probe refusal, application startup logs, the mounted file, and the exact invalid ConfigMap key. A deeper cause for why the bad ConfigMap was created is not observable from Kubernetes runtime data.", "evidenceSufficiency": "Supported by Deployment/ReplicaSet manifests, pod status, current and previous logs, ConfigMap contents, events, Service data, node statistics, and pod metrics. Assumption: the intended remediation is to remove `invalid_directive` or switch to `gateway-config`; the original change author/source is unverified. Alertmanager and Prometheus discovery requests timed out.", "regressionAwareness": "Resource context reports no Tier 1 or Tier 2 remediation history and `regression_detected: false`; this approach therefore does not repeat a known failed remediation.", "alternativeHypotheses": "Considered probe misconfiguration, image pull failure, OOM/resource exhaustion, node failure, and Service configuration. Probe failures are downstream of the application exit; image pull and scheduling succeeded; logs show exit code 1 rather than OOMKilled; application usage was low; all failing pods share the same bad ConfigMap while the prior template is healthy; Service port/selector are consistent. Node disk/CPU pressure remains a contributing operational risk but does not explain the identical invalid-directive log.", "confidenceCalibration": "0.97: reduced from 1.0 by 0.02 because the provenance of the bad ConfigMap change was not available and by 0.01 because Prometheus/Alertmanager queries timed out. Direct logs, ConfigMap diff, pod status, and healthy previous ReplicaSet provide strong confirmation."}, "remediationTarget": {"kind": "ConfigMap", "name": "gateway-config-bad", "namespace": "demo-ingress"}, "contributingFactors": ["Deployment revision 2 references gateway-config-bad instead of the healthy gateway-config.", "Liveness and readiness probes fail because the application exits before serving port 8080.", "Node filesystem utilization and CPU pressure are present but are not the direct cause."]}, "alternativeWorkflows": [{"rationale": "It also removes the identifiable invalid directive and restarts the Deployment, but its stated risk-tolerance requirement is high and this incident has medium risk tolerance.", "confidence": 0.72, "workflowId": "e8507cdf-73db-5ef4-b056-2203fd4f472d"}]}, "total_prompt_tokens": 71424, "total_completion_tokens": 2332}}, {"event_id":"1a0807f9-091b-4253-8b00-4cbcef1b8465","event_timestamp":"2026-10-02T04:37:48