X**\n- This is a likely contributor to overall control-plane CPU pressure.\n\n#### `etcd-kubernaut-remote-cluster-control-plane`\n- CPU: **~36 mCPU**\n- Memory: **~85–87 MB usage**, **~74–80 MB working set**\n- CPU PSI: **~11–12% over 5m**\n- I/O PSI some: up to **~0.76% over 5m**\n- Major page faults: **~151K**, notably high relative to its memory footprint.\n- Network: **~765 MB RX / 230 MB TX**\n\n#### `systemContainers/pods`\n- Aggregate pods container:\n  - CPU: **318 mCPU**\n  - Memory: **2.78 GB usage**, **2.01 GB working set**\n  - CPU PSI: **full 19.25% / some 25.72% over 5m**\n- This indicates broad pod-level CPU scheduling contention rather than an isolated application spike.\n\n#### Runtime system container\n- CPU: **~105 mCPU**\n- CPU PSI: **full 6.05% / some 7.98% over 5m**\n- Memory: **~285 MB usage**, **269 MB working set**\n- No meaningful memory PSI.\n\n### Other notable workloads\n- `kube-mcp-server`:\n  - Highest observed pod network volume: **~1.58 GB RX / 359 MB TX**\n  - CPU ~**45 mCPU**, memory ~**102 MB**\n  - Moderate CPU PSI, but no meaningful memory pressure.\n- `cert-manager-controller`:\n  - CPU ~**10.7 mCPU**\n  - CPU PSI elevated over 5m: **~10%**\n  - Memory ~**95 MB usage**\n- `kube-controller-manager`:\n  - CPU ~**27–32 mCPU**\n  - CPU PSI ~**9–11% over 5m**\n  - Memory ~**135–155 MB**\n- `kube-proxy`, CoreDNS, metrics-server, and local-path-provisioner show moderate CPU PSI but low memory pressure.\n- Demo workloads (`api-gateway`, `checkout-api`, `worker`) are individually small and do not appear to be primary resource drivers.\n- Several application pods have recent starts around **04:39–04:40 UTC**, but the supplied data does not establish whether this reflects normal rollout activity or incident-related restarts.\n\n### Network\n- Node aggregate:\n  - **765 MB RX**\n  - **230 MB TX**\n  - **0 errors**\n- Traffic totals appear heavily associated with Kubernetes control-plane/system traffic; no evidence of packet loss is present in this snapshot.\n\n### Process and limits\n- Node process count: **2,536**\n- PID limit: **111,179**\n- No immediate PID exhaustion indicated.\n\n## Investigation priorities\n\n1. **Investigate CPU contention first**\n   - Correlate node CPU saturation with `kube-apiserver`, etcd, Prometheus, and control-plane request rates.\n   - Check CPU limits/requests and whether the node is oversubscribed.\n   - Review scheduler latency, API-server request latency, etcd fsync/commit latency, and throttling metrics.\n\n2. **Investigate disk usage urgently**\n   - The node has only **~2.97 GB free (~3%)**.\n   - Identify host-level consumers not represented by container filesystem usage: container logs, image layers, journal, deleted-open files, and local-path volumes.\n   - Check for imminent `DiskPressure`, eviction, image-pull failures, or etcd/Prometheus write failures.\n\n3. **Validate control-plane health**\n   - Examine etcd WAL/database growth and disk latency.\n   - Review API-server audit/request logs and etcd health/leader metrics.\n   - The combination of elevated control-plane CPU PSI and nearly full disk could cause API latency or cascading pod failures.\n\n4. **Review Prometheus ingestion/storage**\n   - Prometheus has sustained CPU pressure and ~471 MB of local DB/ephemeral usage in the snapshot.\n   - Check scrape volume, WAL growth, compaction activity, retention, and remote-write behavior.\n\n5. **Check recent pod restarts/rollouts**\n   - Multiple pods, particularly demo ingress pods and system components, have start times close to the collection window.\n   - Confirm whether these are expected deployments or symptoms of resource/disk pressure.", "tool_arguments": {"name": "kubernaut-remote-cluster-control-plane"}, "tool_call_index": 9, "tool_result_preview": "## Incident summary — node `kubernaut-remote-cluster-control-plane`\n\n*Output was pre-truncated; findings apply only to the supplied portion.*\n\n### Overall state\n- Metrics captured around **2026-