 supports dynamic pool expansion via restart.\n", "whenNotToUse": "When the exhaustion is caused solely by a connection leak that will re-saturate any higher limit — scale down or restart the leaking workload instead. Also avoid when the database is at its hardware memory ceiling and cannot support more connections.\n", "preconditions": "A ConfigMap holds the POSTGRESQL_MAX_CONNECTIONS value, the database Deployment reads it via envFrom, and the database has sufficient memory headroom to handle the increased connection count.\n"}, "workflowName": "increase-db-connections-v1"}, {"name": "patch-configuration-v1", "version": "1.6.3", "workflowId": "5abcef80-b22d-5c17-ae27-e9949cf8fb1e", "description": {"what": "Patches a ConfigMap to remove invalid keys or restore known-good values from a backup ConfigMap, then restarts the Deployment that mounts it to recover from configuration-induced crashes", "whenToUse": "When a Deployment's pods are crashing because a mounted ConfigMap contains invalid data (bad directives, corrupted values, injected keys) and the Deployment spec itself has NOT changed — only the ConfigMap content was modified in-place. Look for: (1) CrashLoopBackOff with exit code 1 and startup errors referencing config files, (2) the Deployment's volume still references the same ConfigMap name as before, (3) the ConfigMap data contains keys or values that were recently added or modified. This is the correct workflow when the root cause is bad ConfigMap content, not a bad Deployment spec change.", "whenNotToUse": "When the Deployment spec was changed to reference a different ConfigMap name (e.g. volumes[].configMap.name was modified) — use CrashloopRollback instead, which reverts the entire pod template. When the crash is caused by an image change, resource limits, or external dependency failure. When the ConfigMap is managed by a GitOps tool (ArgoCD/Flux) — use GitRevert instead.", "preconditions": "The ConfigMap exists in the target namespace. Either a backup ConfigMap (<name>-backup, <name>-good, <name>-original) is available, or the bad keys can be identified and removed."}, "workflowName": "patch-configuration-v1"}, {"name": "hotfix-config-v1", "version": "1.6.3", "workflowId": "e8507cdf-73db-5ef4-b056-2203fd4f472d", "description": {"what": "Patches a faulty ConfigMap in-place to remove an invalid configuration directive, then restarts the owning Deployment so pods load the corrected configuration", "whenToUse": "When a Deployment enters CrashLoopBackOff due to an identifiable configuration error in a mounted ConfigMap and the signal carries risk_tolerance=high. The target resource may be the ConfigMap itself or the Deployment that mounts it. Prefer this over rollback when the ConfigMap content is the root cause, because a deployment rollback only masks the problem -- the poisoned ConfigMap remains in the cluster and will re-trigger crashes if pods are rescheduled. In-place patching eliminates the root cause permanently. Best for staging environments or teams that accept the risk of modifying live resources for faster, definitive recovery.", "whenNotToUse": "When the environment is GitOps-managed (ArgoCD/Flux detected) -- an in-place patch will be overwritten by the next GitOps sync, use GitRevertCommit instead. Also do not use when risk_tolerance=low, when the configuration error cannot be identified programmatically, or when a clean rollback to a known-good revision is preferred for maximum safety.", "preconditions": "A Deployment mounts a ConfigMap as a volume, and the ConfigMap contains an identifiable invalid directive that can be removed to restore health."}, "workflowName": "hotfix-config-v1"}], "actionType": "PatchConfiguration"}, "tool_arguments": {"action_type": "PatchConfiguration"}, "tool_call_index": 0, "tool_result_preview": "{\"actionType\":\"PatchConfiguration\",\"workflows\":[{\"workflowId\":\"32f9c2e8-e3a6-57a4-9ee2-6cafdea7d4dd\",\"workflowName\":\"increase-db-connections-v1\",\"name\":\"increase-db-connections-v1\",\"description\":{\"what\":\"Increases the PostgreSQL max_connections limit by patching the database ConfigMap and restarting the database Deployment, relieving connection pool exhaustion so blocked clients can reconnect.\\n\",\"whenToUse\":\"When a DatabaseConnectionPoolExhausted or similar alert indicates that active database "}}, {"event_id":"01e30be3-6965-44d4-a812-4d092f44a6a2","event_timestamp":"2026-10-02T04:42:21+00:00","event_type":"aiagent.llm.request","event_action":"llm_request","event_outcome":"success","correlation_id":"rr-4309bc63be09-59d3c641","event_data":{"model": "gpt-5.6-luna", "phase": "workflow_discovery", "event_id": "24f936fd-74f2-4ccc-9f8b-f7b4fb3a04a2", "event_type": "aiagent.llm.request", "incident_id": "rr-4309bc63be09-59d3c641", "prompt_length": 26639, "prompt_preview": "RCA findings: The three crashing api-gateway replicas use `gateway-config-bad`, whose `invalid_directive: true` causes `demo-http-server:1.0.0` to exit with code 1 during startup; the Deployment template must be corrected to mount `gateway-config`.\n\nSelect the appropriate remediation workflow.", "toolsets_enabled": ["list_available_actions", "list_workflows", "get_workflow", "todo_write", "submit_result_with_workflow", "submit_result_no_workflow"], "workflow_discovery_context": {"prompt_labels_present": false, "signal_labels_present": true, "enrichment_labels_present": false}}}, {"event_id":"8f7f9094-c6f1-4d4c-b4aa-9a08e92c07a7","event_timestamp":"2026-10-02T04:42:21+00:00","event_type":"aiagent.llm.response","event_action":"llm_response","event_outcome":"success","correlation_id":"rr-4309bc63be09-59d3c641","event_data":{"event_id": "0355de79-b786-4770-9910-ce73f1f3b0c0", "event_type": "aiagent.llm.response", "incident_id": "rr-4309bc63be09-59d3c641", "tokens_used": 6590, "has_analysis": false, "analysis_length": 0, "tool_call_count": 1, "analysis_preview": ""}}, {"event_id":"ba6a1e07-b55e-415d-85f4-1f3e6bfa8912","event_timestamp":"2026-10-02T04:42:21+